IX. Gilgamesh går ad solens vej
IX. Gilgamesh takes the road of the sun

Gilgamesh græd bittert over sin ven Enkidu, mens han strejfede over steppen. »Skal jeg ikke også dø som Enkidu? Sorgen har fundet vej til mit hjerte. Jeg er bange for at dø, mens jeg strejfer over steppen. Jeg vil opsøge Utnapishtim, Ubara-Tutus søn. Jeg vil skynde mig af sted. Hvis jeg kommer til bjergportene i mørke og møder løver dér, bliver jeg bange. Så vil jeg løfte hovedet og bede til måneguden, eller […] guderne høre min bøn […] Red mig!« Han sov […] og så en drøm […] de frydede sig over livet. Han svang øksen […] i hånden, trak sværdet af bæltet og sprang ind imellem dem som et spyd […] slog […] knuste […]. [Resten af spalten er beskadiget.]
Gilgamesh wept bitterly for his friend Enkidu as he roamed the steppe. “Won't I too die like Enkidu? Sorrow has entered my heart. I'm afraid of dying as I roam the steppe. I'll seek out Utnapishtim, son of Ubara-Tutu. I'll travel quickly. If I reach the mountain gates in darkness and meet lions there, I'll be afraid. Then I'll lift my head and pray to the moon god, or […] let the gods hear my prayer […] Save me!” He slept […] and saw a dream […] they rejoiced in life. He raised the axe […] in his hand, drew the sword from his belt and sprang among them like a spear […] striking […] crushing […]. [The remainder of the column is damaged.]

Bjergene hedder Mashu. Gilgamesh nåede Mashu, hvor de hver dag holder vagt ved solgudens opgang og nedgang. Toppene når himlens højeste punkt, og dybt ned i dødsriget når bjergenes bryst. Ved porten står skorpionmenneskene på vagt. De indgyder rædsel. Deres blik betyder døden. Deres vælde får bjergene til at skælve. De vogter Shamash, både når han går op, og når han går ned. Da Gilgamesh så dem, blev han bleg af skræk. Han kastede sig til jorden foran dem, ude af sig selv.
The mountains are called Mashu. Gilgamesh reached Mashu, where every day they keep watch over the sun god's rising and setting. Their peaks reach the highest point of heaven and their breasts extend deep into the underworld. Scorpion people stand guard at the gate. They are terrifying. Their glance means death. Their majesty makes the mountains shake. They guard Shamash both as he rises and as he sets. At the sight of them Gilgamesh turned pale with fear and threw himself to the ground before them, beside himself.

Skorpionmanden sagde til sin kone: »Se ham, der kommer imod os. Hans krop er af gudernes kød.« Hans kone svarede: »To dele af ham er gud. Kun den tredje er menneske.« [Otte brudte linjer følger. Skorpionmanden spørger, hvorfor Gilgamesh er rejst så langt, og beskriver den svære vej. Gilgamesh vil spørge Utnapishtim om liv og død. Ingen har nogensinde krydset bjergene, svarer skorpionmanden. Solens vej tager tolv dobbelttimer og begynder i dybt mørke. Omtrent en spalte mangler.]
The scorpion man said to his wife, “Look at the one coming towards us. His body is made of the flesh of gods.” His wife answered, “Two parts of him are divine. Only the third is human.” [Eight broken lines follow. The scorpion man asks why Gilgamesh has travelled so far and describes the difficult passage. Gilgamesh wants to ask Utnapishtim about life and death. No one has ever crossed the mountains, the scorpion man answers. The road of the sun takes twelve double hours and begins in deep darkness. About a column is missing.]

»Om det så gør ondt […] om kulde og hede bider i mit ansigt, og jeg må gå i sorg […].« [Skorpionmanden nævner Mashu og ønsker Gilgamesh held på rejsen.] Da Gilgamesh hørte skorpionmandens ord, drog han af sted ad solens vej […]. [De første to timer går han i fuldstændigt mørke, så han ikke kan se bag sig. For hver dobbelttime frem til og med den ottende er det lige mørkt. I den niende aner han tilsyneladende lys. Ved udgangen af den tolvte står han i fuld sol. Foran ham er gudernes træ.]
“Even if it hurts […] even if cold and heat wear my face and I must walk in sorrow […].” [The scorpion man mentions Mashu and wishes Gilgamesh success.] Hearing the scorpion man's words, Gilgamesh set off along the road of the sun […]. [The first two hours are utterly dark. He cannot see behind him. Each double hour through the eighth is just as dark. In the ninth he seems to glimpse light. By the end of the twelfth he stands in full sunlight. Before him is the gods' tree.]

Det bar rubinrøde frugter. Rankerne hang ned, smukke at se på. Løvet lyste blåt. Dets frugter var en fryd for øjet. [Sjette spalte er næsten helt gået tabt. Den oldbabylonske version fortæller videre:] »Han har beredt de vilde dyrs skind og spiser deres kød. Gilgamesh, der bliver aldrig nogen overgang, hvor der aldrig har været en før [?], så længe stormen driver vandet foran sig.«
It bore ruby-red fruit. Tendrils hung down, lovely to see. Its foliage shone blue. Its fruit was a delight to the eye. [The sixth column is nearly all lost. The Old Babylonian version continues:] “He has dressed the wild animals' skins and eats their flesh. Gilgamesh, there will never be a crossing where none has been before [?], so long as the gale drives the water.”

Shamash fik medlidenhed og kaldte på ham: »Gilgamesh, hvorfor løber du? Det liv, du søger, finder du aldrig.« Gilgamesh svarede krigeren Shamash: »Skal jeg først strejfe hjemløs gennem ødemarken og siden lægge mit hoved i jordens indre og sove der år efter år, for evigt? Lad mine øjne se solen og mættes af lyset. Hvor lyset breder sig, viger mørket langt bort. Hvornår skal den, der er død, nogensinde se sollyset igen?« [Den assyriske version slutter med omkring tolv brudte linjer, der nævner sten og mineraler.]
Shamash took pity and called to him, “Gilgamesh, why are you running? The life you're seeking you will never find.” Gilgamesh answered the warrior Shamash, “Must I roam the wilderness as a wanderer and then lay my head deep in the earth and sleep there year after year, for ever? Let my eyes see the sun and drink in its brightness. Where the light spreads wide, darkness retreats far away. When will anyone who is dead ever see the light of the sun again?” [The Assyrian version ends with about twelve damaged lines mentioning stones and minerals.]
